= Turing machine =

== Overview ==
A '''Turing machine''' is an abstract model of computation consisting of finite control, a read/write head, a tape divided into cells, and transition rules.

Alan Turing introduced the model in 1936 to analyze effective calculation and limits of computation.

== Components ==
A standard Turing machine has:

* a finite set of states;
* a finite tape alphabet;
* an idealized unbounded tape;
* a head that reads and writes one tape cell;
* a transition function;
* start, accepting, or halting states depending on formulation.

== Computation ==
At each step, the machine reads the current symbol, changes state, writes a symbol, and moves the head left or right according to its transition rule.

Despite this simplicity, Turing machines can express all algorithms captured by standard equivalent models of effective computation.

== Universal Turing machine ==
A universal Turing machine can simulate other Turing machines when given an encoding of the machine and its input.

This provides a formal basis for general-purpose programmable computation.

== Church–Turing thesis ==
The Church–Turing thesis states that effectively calculable functions are exactly those computable by a Turing machine or equivalent formal model.

It is not a mathematical theorem derived from a prior formal definition of “effective calculability”; it connects an informal concept with formal models.

== Limits ==
Turing machines cannot solve every precisely stated problem.

The halting problem is undecidable.

This limitation applies to every computational model equivalent in power to Turing machines.

== Physical computers ==
A Turing machine is an idealized mathematical model, not a literal description of modern hardware.

Real computers have finite resources.

